<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</h1><p class="st-meta">1856 CE – 1970 CE · capital Muscat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Oman's Al Bu Said sultanate after the loss of Zanzibar, a coastal state in tension with the interior imams until the 1970 coup.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said ibn Sultan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a> died in 1856, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuwaini-bin-said">Thuwaini</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and another son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majid-bin-said">Majid</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning-award">Canning Award</a> of 1861, arbitrated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, confirmed the split, with Zanzibar paying Muscat an annual subsidy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cut off from East African wealth and steamship trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> declined and depended on British support. The interior Ibadi tribes resented the coastal sultans; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzan-ibn-qais">Azzan ibn Qais</a> briefly held Muscat as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> in 1868-1871, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a> rose in 1913, kept at bay by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb">Treaty of Seeb</a> in 1920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a>, sultan from 1932, kept the country isolated, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war">Jebel Akhdar War</a> of 1954-1959 and sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwadar">Gwadar</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1958.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Oil exports began in 1967 but development stayed slow while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> rebellion grew. In July 1970 Said's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaboos-bin-said">Qaboos</a> deposed him and renamed the country the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-oman">Sultanate of Oman</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Muscat and Oman" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Muscat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1856 CE – 1970 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muscat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thuwaini ibn Said, Faisal ibn Turki, Said bin Taimur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muscat and Oman, Sultanate of Muscat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1913&amp;focus=sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman" aria-label="Sultanate of Muscat and Oman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6242 1307 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Muscat and Oman lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6562 1563L6562 1563M6562 1563L4997 651M6562 1563L6562 1563M6562 1563L6537 1587"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6562 1563h0M4997 651h0M6562 1563h0M6537 1587h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6562 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6562 1563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Muscat. <a href="/atlas?year=1913&amp;focus=sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
